&lt;p&gt;💡&lt;strong&gt;Pro Tip:&lt;/strong&gt; Stack two AI assistants for code review and bug prediction. You’ll catch issues even the vendor didn’t anticipate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How much can AI cut software development costs in 2026?AI adoption saves the average software team 28% in development costs by 2026, according to McKinsey. Most savings come from faster bug detection and reduced manual testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Will AI replace software developers in the future?AI won’t fully replace developers by 2026, but it will automate up to 60% of routine coding tasks. Developers who adapt to AI-assisted workflows will thrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What skills should developers focus on for the AI-driven future?Critical thinking, prompt engineering, and architectural design are the top skills for 2026. Pure syntax memorization is losing value as AI handles more coding fundamentals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which AI tools are most recommended for software teams in 2026?GitHub Copilot Workspace, Tabnine Pro, and DeepCode are the most recommended AI tools for code generation, multi-language support, and security review in 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The future is uncomfortable—get comfortable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI isn’t coming for your job. It’s coming for the boring half of your job. You’ll notice the best teams in 2026 are the ones who hand off grunt work, automate the repeatable, and double down on what only humans can do: asking better questions. That’s the real future of AI in software development. And it’s already late.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;More articles at &lt;a href="https://nlocoding.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nlocoding.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google Search Console has introduced a &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/gemini/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;multimodal search type filter&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in its Performance reports, giving website owners a new way to see how content surfaces when a Google search begins with an image rather than typed text. The reporting groups traffic from Google Lens, Circle to Search on Android, image uploads to Google Search, and Chrome's right-click image search.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The addition matters because visual input can start a search journey differently from a conventional keyword query. Rather than treating image-led discovery as invisible or scattered across reporting, Search Console now provides a cross-channel view of this activity. Google said the feature began rolling out globally on September 24, 2026, and sites should begin to see data as traffic from these image-based searches arrives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;According to &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2026/09/web-multimodal-in-sc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google's official announcement of web multimodal Search performance reporting&lt;/a&gt;, the filter is available in both the standard Search results Performance report and the Generative AI features report. This makes the update relevant to publishers, ecommerce sites, local businesses, and any team that uses Search Console to understand how people find their pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the multimodal filter reports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The filter covers searches where a user initiates Google Search with visual input. It is not a report for every image on a site, nor does it turn image-search activity into a separate analytics product. It is a new search-type view inside existing Search Console Performance reporting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google identifies four included entry points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Google Lens&lt;/strong&gt; searches initiated from an image.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Circle to Search&lt;/strong&gt; queries on Android.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Image uploads&lt;/strong&gt; to Google Search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chrome's right-click &lt;strong&gt;“Search this image”&lt;/strong&gt; feature.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This grouping is important for interpretation. The announced filter combines these image-initiated pathways under the multimodal search type, offering an aggregate view of visual search behavior rather than separate filters for each named entry point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Performance report&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Multimodal filter availability&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it covers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search results&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Available&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Performance data from searches initiated with images through the included Google entry points&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generative AI features&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Available&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multimodal performance reporting within Google's Generative AI features report&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How to find the new data
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Website owners can access the information by opening a relevant Performance report in Search Console and applying the &lt;strong&gt;multimodal search type filter&lt;/strong&gt;. The interface location matters because this is an extension of the reporting workflow teams already use, not a new standalone dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As with any new global rollout, the first useful task is simply to establish whether a property is receiving measurable traffic through the new filter. Google has said metrics will start appearing as traffic from these image-based queries reaches a site. A lack of immediate data should therefore not be treated as proof that images never contribute to discovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What changes for SEO and content workflows
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The central change is measurement. Teams can now separate a combined set of image-initiated search interactions from other Search Console traffic and assess whether that channel is meaningful for their site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That can support a more focused review of pages that already receive multimodal visibility. For example, a team can compare the filtered view with its broader Search Console reporting, identify which content appears in the data, and decide whether visual assets deserve closer editorial or merchandising attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The announcement does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; prescribe a new optimization checklist, guarantee additional traffic, or provide a split between Lens, Circle to Search, uploaded images, and Chrome image search. It also does not establish that every image on a website is eligible to surface in these journeys. The practical value comes from observing the reported data before changing a workflow or allocating more resources to visual content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For businesses, that is a useful distinction. Image-led search may be especially relevant where people need to identify, compare, or learn about something visible, but Search Console data should guide decisions rather than assumptions about audience behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


A practical way to use the filter
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sensible starting point is to treat multimodal reporting as a recurring measurement view, not a one-time novelty. Review it alongside the pages and content areas already central to a site's search strategy. If the filter reveals consistent activity, document the affected URLs and use that evidence when planning content updates or visual asset reviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the analysis narrow at first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm that multimodal data is appearing for the property.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the pages associated with that activity in the Performance report.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare the filtered view with broader search performance to understand its relative role.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revisit the report over time as the global rollout matures and more traffic data accumulates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach avoids overstating what a new reporting dimension can prove. It also gives marketing and web teams a shared evidence base for discussing visual search without conflating it with every form of image performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/google-lens-25-billion-monthly-searches-visual-search/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Visual search reporting&lt;/a&gt; creates a useful signal, but teams still need a clear picture of where their brand appears across changing search experiences. Scalevise can help turn that visibility into a practical measurement plan through its &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/ai-visibility-geo-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Visibility / GEO Checker&lt;/a&gt;, helping you identify opportunities and prioritize the pages that matter. &lt;strong&gt;Start an AI Visibility scan to understand your current &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/geo/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;search presence&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the Google Search Console multimodal filter?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a new search type filter in Search Console Performance reports that shows data from searches initiated with images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which image-based Google search features are included?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google includes Google Lens, Circle to Search on Android, image uploads to Google Search, and Chrome's right-click “Search this image” feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where can website owners use the multimodal filter?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The filter is available in both the standard Search results Performance report and the Generative AI features report in Google Search Console.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does the filter show separate data for Lens and Circle to Search?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google describes the feature as a combined multimodal search type filter covering the listed image-initiated entry points. The announcement does not describe separate filters for each entry point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When will multimodal performance data appear in Search Console?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google said the feature began rolling out globally on September 24, 2026. Data should begin appearing as a site receives traffic from covered image-based queries.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google's multimodal filter gives Search Console users a defined way to measure a growing category of image-initiated search activity. Its immediate value is visibility: website owners can determine whether Google Lens, Circle to Search, image uploads, and Chrome image search contribute measurable discovery for their content. The most useful next step is to monitor the new filter over time and let the reported data, rather than assumptions, shape any response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/sanity-2026-09-16"&gt;Sanity Challenge, Path Two: Vibe-Code Something Strange&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Incident War Room&lt;/strong&gt; is an incident-response app for on-call teams. When&lt;br&gt;
something breaks at 2 AM, everything about the incident lives on one page:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;live timeline&lt;/strong&gt; that every responder sees update in real time, no refresh&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;SEV1 escalation gate&lt;/strong&gt;: raising an incident to SEV1 needs sign-off from
&lt;strong&gt;two different on-call leads&lt;/strong&gt; before anything changes, and it's enforced
on the server, not just hidden in the UI&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;status page entry&lt;/strong&gt; published automatically once the escalation is approved&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;runbooks&lt;/strong&gt; for the incident's severity shown right on the page&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;AI-drafted postmortem&lt;/strong&gt;: resolving freezes the timeline and an LLM
drafts the root cause and action items from it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;Ops Dashboard&lt;/strong&gt; inside Sanity Studio, built with the App SDK, that
can approve escalations too, through the same gate&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's built with Next.js 16 (App Router) and Sanity as the whole backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fz6p776cs526umh6m9pdv.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fz6p776cs526umh6m9pdv.png" alt="Incidents list" width="800" height="475"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live app:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://incident-war-room-nine.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://incident-war-room-nine.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/5JVUoHkKi0c" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try the gate yourself&lt;/strong&gt; (all passwords are &lt;code&gt;demo1234&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Log in as &lt;code&gt;alice@example.com&lt;/code&gt; (prefilled), open the sample incident and click &lt;strong&gt;Raise to SEV1&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Approve as Alice. It stays at &lt;strong&gt;1 / 2&lt;/strong&gt; and the incident is still SEV3.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Log out&lt;/strong&gt;, log in as &lt;code&gt;dana@example.com&lt;/code&gt;, open the same incident and approve. It flips to SEV1 / escalated and a status page entry appears.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Mark resolved&lt;/strong&gt;. The timeline is frozen into a postmortem with an AI-drafted root cause and action items.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Log in as &lt;code&gt;bob@example.com&lt;/code&gt; (an engineer): you can't approve at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F03i97sh6w4qghwlnezw4.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F03i97sh6w4qghwlnezw4.png" alt="One of two approvals: the incident is still SEV3" width="800" height="475"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flvte898c9ipbbculacq8.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flvte898c9ipbbculacq8.png" alt="Escalated, published to the status page, resolved, and the AI-drafted postmortem" width="799" height="562"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

